1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,267
We're going to talk about, I guess,
one of your favorite subjects,

2
00:00:03,333 --> 00:00:07,200
the PEN&PAD system Could
you tell us a little bit about

3
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:11,467
who you are, your background, how did
you get into this, what was the ideas

4
00:00:11,533 --> 00:00:15,367
leading to this PEN&PAD electronic
health record system, things like that.

5
00:00:17,133 --> 00:00:22,333
Yes, well PEN&PAD grew out of a long
interest in medical decision-making.

6
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:29,233
I'd come out of an original background in
philosophy and mathematics and foundations

7
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:35,300
and logic and then medical school
and with various things in between,

8
00:00:35,367 --> 00:00:38,867
but I turned out to be much better
at computer science than medicine

9
00:00:39,467 --> 00:00:46,467
and came over to the UK to work with Tim
de Dombal on computerized decision-making,

10
00:00:46,533 --> 00:00:49,967
in the days of Bayesian
decision-making and idiot Bayes,

11
00:00:51,133 --> 00:00:57,333
and then ended up, through a long series
of things which I won't bore people with,

12
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:01,000
in a very innovative department
of general practice and community

13
00:01:01,067 --> 00:01:05,767
medicine at University of Nottingham, just
at the time when computers were suddenly

14
00:01:05,833 --> 00:01:11,133
becoming something one could imagine
working with and we set up a system

15
00:01:12,733 --> 00:01:16,767
for GPs and I spent a lot of time
investigating it but it was an interesting

16
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:23,833
department that had a mixture of doctors
and sociologists and psychologists,

17
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:30,500
eventually we got to the point where there
was the big panic about expert systems

18
00:01:30,567 --> 00:01:35,800
and the Japanese fifth generation project.
And that was enough to panic the research

19
00:01:35,867 --> 00:01:39,467
councils into funding research
on medical decision-making.

20
00:01:40,467 --> 00:01:42,900
And we had a very naive project,

21
00:01:42,967 --> 00:01:47,033
naive in retrospect, we didn't think
it was naive when we proposed it,

22
00:01:47,633 --> 00:01:51,900
of developing a whole series
of expert systems to help GPs.

23
00:01:53,133 --> 00:01:57,800
We were clever enough to at least
have a group of psychologists

24
00:01:57,867 --> 00:02:03,900
and sociologists and GPs working with us,
a lot of studies then, in Nottingham,

25
00:02:03,967 --> 00:02:07,500
of observing GPs actually
having actors play.

26
00:02:07,567 --> 00:02:11,800
the role of patients,
in some cases, looking at things.

27
00:02:11,867 --> 00:02:16,567
And we ended up making the decision
that what we were trying to do

28
00:02:16,633 --> 00:02:21,100
was even if we could do it,
which we obviously couldn't, was bonkers,

29
00:02:21,167 --> 00:02:23,833
and wasn't going
to be of any use to anybody,

30
00:02:24,767 --> 00:02:28,267
and started to think about
how else you would do it.

31
00:02:28,333 --> 00:02:32,800
And at the end of that project,
we mocked up a piece

32
00:02:32,867 --> 00:02:38,467
of cardboard that looked sort
of like the PEN&PAD interface.

33
00:02:39,867 --> 00:02:46,733
It was literally cardboard, it was done
in display post script, on a Sun,

34
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:52,833
but it was convincing enough to get
one person on the committee to ask,

35
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:57,900
haven't you done the project? People were
very naive about computing at that point.

36
00:02:58,767 --> 00:03:05,600
We got a report out of that project which
was gave in which half the reviewers gave

37
00:03:05,667 --> 00:03:10,967
us five stars for having this and half
of the reviewers gave us one.

38
00:03:12,833 --> 00:03:17,100
the one star for not having done
what we said we were going to do,

39
00:03:17,167 --> 00:03:20,867
five stars for having suggested
something more interesting.

40
00:03:20,933 --> 00:03:23,367
But out of a lot of discussion,

41
00:03:23,433 --> 00:03:27,133
eventually we got a joint project
with the Department of Health to build

42
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:33,267
what became PEN&PAD and put together
what was really quite an amazing team.

43
00:03:34,300 --> 00:03:38,967
And in the meantime, for reasons
which are complicated, I moved to,

44
00:03:39,033 --> 00:03:43,667
and David Metcalfe who led the GP
group had both moved to Manchester.

45
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:51,000
And so we put together a group
to try and do the same kind of study?

46
00:03:51,067 --> 00:03:54,567
That's what might be useful in Manchester.

47
00:03:54,633 --> 00:04:00,633
And really, what came out
of this was both a plan for how you do

48
00:04:01,267 --> 00:04:06,200
user-centered design and general practice.
And general practice in the UK, at least,

49
00:04:06,267 --> 00:04:10,900
was easier than many other areas
of medicine because general practitioners

50
00:04:10,967 --> 00:04:15,800
sat at desks, and were much easier
to study than hospital doctors

51
00:04:15,867 --> 00:04:21,767
who constantly moved around, or nurses
who ran around even faster and so —

52
00:04:21,833 --> 00:04:25,167
And you didn't have very much mobile
computers at that time, right?

53
00:04:25,867 --> 00:04:27,967
The standard of mobility,

54
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:37,600
Our first, quote, laptop was described
as the Arnold Schwarzenegger laptop,

55
00:04:37,867 --> 00:04:44,200
which we took to Brussels at the end
of the first stage of PEN&PAD

56
00:04:44,267 --> 00:04:46,300
when we were applying for GALEN,

57
00:04:46,367 --> 00:04:52,200
which was a huge 25-pound
Toshiba monstrosity,

58
00:04:52,833 --> 00:04:57,267
which the airlines weren't even very happy
in and putting in an overhead bin for us

59
00:04:57,333 --> 00:04:58,733
and wanted to check.

60
00:04:59,433 --> 00:05:04,900
Anthony Nowlan, who some of you may know,
fought with them over hand-carrying this,

61
00:05:04,967 --> 00:05:08,600
which obviously we didn't wish
to trust in the baggage department.

62
00:05:10,733 --> 00:05:15,667
But it was a very busy program

63
00:05:15,733 --> 00:05:20,533
of on the one hand user centered
design, working with the doctors.

64
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:23,467
And that's what we're gonna…
the user-centered design will

65
00:05:23,533 --> 00:05:27,100
dive into deeper in section
two of this recording, I guess.

66
00:05:27,167 --> 00:05:31,633
Yeah, and we'll talk
about more of that later.

67
00:05:31,700 --> 00:05:36,667
And then also, this was just
the time when there was a lot of work

68
00:05:38,033 --> 00:05:42,500
on knowledge representation.

69
00:05:42,567 --> 00:05:47,967
The ideas behind description logic had
not actually been invented quite yet.

70
00:05:48,033 --> 00:05:51,833
The projects that were going into
description logic were happening.

71
00:05:52,367 --> 00:05:54,167
And we picked that up

72
00:05:54,233 --> 00:05:58,333
and picked up the basic tool
and algorithm that I'll talk about

73
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,800
in the third part of the talk
as our Swiss Army knife, the thing

74
00:06:04,867 --> 00:06:09,600
that turned out to be useful for all sorts
of things and we still think is useful.

75
00:06:10,333 --> 00:06:14,067
And that worked very well.

76
00:06:14,667 --> 00:06:21,267
And then that led on to just
a whole series of projects.

77
00:06:22,733 --> 00:06:27,067
some of them in knowledge representation,
some of them in bioinformatics,

78
00:06:27,133 --> 00:06:31,233
to GALEN above all, obviously,
which is where you met us,

79
00:06:32,133 --> 00:06:39,000
and to eventually the other project,
which I just want to mention briefly

80
00:06:39,067 --> 00:06:43,700
at the end of the talk,
is the ICD-11 project,

81
00:06:43,767 --> 00:06:50,767
which was eventually a project
to coordinate SNOMED and ICD,

82
00:06:51,133 --> 00:06:54,000
which eventually came apart over politics.

83
00:06:55,033 --> 00:06:58,067
Getting any one organization,
all the parts of any one

84
00:06:58,133 --> 00:07:03,400
organization to keep going in a straight
line for three years is difficult,

85
00:07:03,467 --> 00:07:08,400
getting parts of three organizations,
maybe four, to go in the same direction,

86
00:07:08,467 --> 00:07:13,600
eventually we weren't, I at least and the
people around me weren't clever enough

87
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:20,033
to pull off quite.
So it never quite actually…

88
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:22,600
eventually ICD decided that they were more

89
00:07:22,667 --> 00:07:25,333
concerned with stability than
they were with innovation.

90
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:29,567
OK, so it didn't stop because
of technical problems there.

91
00:07:29,633 --> 00:07:34,733
It wasn't technical.
No, we were doing very well technically.

92
00:07:35,933 --> 00:07:40,367
It wasn't finished, but the prototypes
were looking very good indeed.

93
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:47,000
But at that point, SNOMED

94
00:07:47,067 --> 00:07:49,233
also underwent a reorganization.

95
00:07:49,867 --> 00:07:51,800
And that was the point
at which Kent Spackman,

96
00:07:51,867 --> 00:07:54,967
who had been leading
it for SNOMED, left SNOMED.

97
00:07:55,733 --> 00:08:00,367
And so it came apart for all
of those kinds of reasons,

98
00:08:00,433 --> 00:08:02,267
rather than anything else.

99
00:08:02,333 --> 00:08:06,467
But we might talk about how to pick up
these ideas anyway in the fourth part

100
00:08:06,533 --> 00:08:08,033
of the interview here.

101
00:08:08,733 --> 00:08:10,833
We can certainly talk about that.

102
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:15,400
I think we did succeed very well
in showing how you might coordinate them

103
00:08:15,933 --> 00:08:21,500
and why you can't just integrate them.
They don't, won't fit together.

104
00:08:21,567 --> 00:08:25,800
The biggest thing to come out
of that project is how different

105
00:08:26,333 --> 00:08:29,800
the tasks are for on the one hand,

106
00:08:29,867 --> 00:08:34,500
recording information for clinical
purposes and the tasks that ICD

107
00:08:34,567 --> 00:08:39,533
has of classifying it and maintaining
statistics for international purposes.

108
00:08:40,233 --> 00:08:43,933
And you can coordinate
those two activities,

109
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,500
but you can't just mash them together.

110
00:08:47,567 --> 00:08:51,667
And a lot of the criticism of over
the years, there's been any

111
00:08:51,733 --> 00:08:55,900
number of arguments, as you know,
about the relative merits of ICD,

112
00:08:55,967 --> 00:09:01,867
and SNOMED, and Read, and ICPC,
and any number, and for that matter, MeSH,

113
00:09:02,467 --> 00:09:05,833
and all the other various things.

114
00:09:05,900 --> 00:09:11,400
And one of the things that I think most
has come out of not PEN&PAD itself,

115
00:09:11,467 --> 00:09:14,967
but the things at the end of this is:
these are different tasks

116
00:09:15,533 --> 00:09:17,200
and you can coordinate them.

117
00:09:18,767 --> 00:09:22,333
But you need to understand what they're
doing and why they're different.

118
00:09:22,867 --> 00:09:24,167
And is that what you're

119
00:09:24,233 --> 00:09:28,900
talking about using some query languages
and things that has a glue between them?

120
00:09:28,967 --> 00:09:34,033
Yes. If from the point of view of SNOMED,

121
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:38,400
you have to do it with query languages
and not with actually putting things

122
00:09:38,467 --> 00:09:43,267
into the SNOMED, putting everything
into the SNOMED formalism itself.

123
00:09:44,833 --> 00:09:47,633
It looks slightly different.
If I were giving this talk

124
00:09:47,700 --> 00:09:50,800
to an ICD audience,
it would look slightly different.

125
00:09:51,433 --> 00:09:57,933
In which way you have to, which point of
view you're looking at the problem from.

126
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:01,367
I think there are many countries,

127
00:10:02,767 --> 00:10:05,933
and not just SNOMED countries,
but even those trying,

128
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:11,333
aiming to implement ICD-11
who are struggling with these issues,

129
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:16,633
and recommendations for those countries,
I guess, are in high demand.

130
00:10:20,233 --> 00:10:26,333
I think these ideas are important
for the coordination of different

131
00:10:27,133 --> 00:10:29,767
nomenclatures and different terminologies.

132
00:10:30,600 --> 00:10:37,400
And the use of, call it an ontology,
call it a logic-based concept model,

133
00:10:37,467 --> 00:10:42,567
whatever you want to call that central
terminology piece, which is logic-based

134
00:10:42,633 --> 00:10:46,733
with various things which have various
pragmatic requirements on them,

135
00:10:47,233 --> 00:10:52,633
which don't fit necessarily
and can't be expressed conveniently,

136
00:10:53,167 --> 00:10:55,767
can't be expressed at all necessarily,

137
00:10:57,933 --> 00:11:02,767
within in the same set of axioms and logic
structure that you have if you're going

138
00:11:02,833 --> 00:11:07,733
to make SNOMED work the way SNOMED
is supposed to work, or any other,

139
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:12,867
or GALEN, and anything that's based
on those on description logic technology.

140
00:11:13,967 --> 00:11:18,567
We can dive deeper into this in the
technical part and in the future part.

141
00:11:19,933 --> 00:11:25,600
We'll pause it a little bit now
and rig up a demo of this PEN&PAD.

142
00:11:26,267 --> 00:11:31,467
Okay, now we've got the computer here.
This runs on a Windows XP emulator.

143
00:11:31,533 --> 00:11:33,933
What is it that we see
in front of us here, Alan?

144
00:11:34,867 --> 00:11:40,433
Well, this is about the second
stage of the screens

145
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:46,833
that we use to enter a patient encounter.
These were intended to be used by a doctor

146
00:11:47,467 --> 00:11:51,100
on a system that was sitting
on their desk while the patient was there.

147
00:11:51,667 --> 00:11:57,833
A key thing was we went on the one
hand we wanted a concise summary

148
00:11:58,433 --> 00:12:03,100
and this is one form of the summary
there were other things that we did

149
00:12:03,167 --> 00:12:07,967
which were actually much harder to do
at that time than they would be now.

150
00:12:08,033 --> 00:12:13,200
We had the version that we had
originally in Smalltalk was prettier

151
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:15,733
because the graphics were prettier.

152
00:12:16,300 --> 00:12:20,100
But the key thing:
if you wanted to enter information,

153
00:12:20,167 --> 00:12:23,867
you clicked on symptom
and you brought up a form.

154
00:12:23,933 --> 00:12:28,633
So if I click the symptom
here and then I've got this

155
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,667
and you can then… we found it was very

156
00:12:32,733 --> 00:12:37,267
important to have people be able
to select things in various ways

157
00:12:37,333 --> 00:12:41,600
so as a starting point you could
either point at the homunculus

158
00:12:41,667 --> 00:12:47,167
the drawings that we had like
the little icons that we had at the top

159
00:12:48,367 --> 00:12:54,400
or or pick up from the text
lists at the side.

160
00:12:54,467 --> 00:12:57,800
And some doctors liked one
and some doctors liked the other.

161
00:12:59,133 --> 00:13:02,233
The graphics were fun

162
00:13:02,833 --> 00:13:08,367
and they helped sell the system
and get people to enjoy it.

163
00:13:08,433 --> 00:13:13,167
But many people actually use the text
much more than they use the graphics.

164
00:13:14,633 --> 00:13:17,767
They were also slightly worried
that the patients would think

165
00:13:17,833 --> 00:13:23,167
that they weren't clever enough to figure
out what the words meant or something.

166
00:13:23,233 --> 00:13:27,200
There was slight worry about
using icons at that point.

167
00:13:27,267 --> 00:13:30,600
This was in 1992. Mice were new.

168
00:13:30,667 --> 00:13:34,633
Some of the doctors had never met
a mouse when they met this system,

169
00:13:34,700 --> 00:13:41,567
let alone use things
that were mostly graphical.

170
00:13:41,633 --> 00:13:45,067
So there were things like
that that we just didn't know.

171
00:13:46,100 --> 00:13:51,033
If you go and you select the hand, you'll
see that each time you select a part,

172
00:13:51,100 --> 00:13:55,267
it will reduce the number

173
00:13:55,333 --> 00:14:00,033
of possibilities and eventually
you get down to a list of words.

174
00:14:01,667 --> 00:14:04,600
That's just the way people communicate.

175
00:14:04,667 --> 00:14:09,200
And you've got down actually
to the thumb there, nicely located,

176
00:14:09,267 --> 00:14:10,733
and if you pick pain.

177
00:14:11,767 --> 00:14:18,200
So thumb and then pain.
It says thumb pain down here.

178
00:14:18,267 --> 00:14:20,500
Thumb pain, and then you press form.

179
00:14:21,767 --> 00:14:26,300
And that's one more click than we wanted.
We were trying to get the clicks down,

180
00:14:26,367 --> 00:14:30,000
but for technical reasons,
which I cannot remember,

181
00:14:30,067 --> 00:14:37,067
we couldn't get rid of that last click.
Maybe today when you thought about it,

182
00:14:37,333 --> 00:14:41,167
you popped up a form and just
a few things about these forms.

183
00:14:42,733 --> 00:14:45,800
They were meant to be fairly
large and easy to click on.

184
00:14:45,867 --> 00:14:50,600
We didn't, we didn't use the dominant
pattern at the time for forms,

185
00:14:50,667 --> 00:14:53,767
it was radio buttons
and little round buttons

186
00:14:53,833 --> 00:14:57,700
and we wanted things that were big,
that people could hit easily.

187
00:14:58,467 --> 00:15:05,000
And we also wanted people
to be able to turn away at any time.

188
00:15:05,067 --> 00:15:07,833
So the reason there
are “more”-buttons there,

189
00:15:08,433 --> 00:15:12,700
rather than click and press
on present, which was:

190
00:15:12,767 --> 00:15:19,433
if you click on 'Thumb
swelling' 'present',

191
00:15:20,867 --> 00:15:23,667
you just get present.
If you click on 'more…',

192
00:15:23,733 --> 00:15:28,967
you'll get a further form with details.
And here I see text also generated.

193
00:15:29,033 --> 00:15:34,967
And it's also generated some text.
And if you click there, you'll get,

194
00:15:35,033 --> 00:15:38,433
and another form comes up.
It isn't something you have to hold on.

195
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:42,367
It's not a pull-down. It's something
that pops up and stays there.

196
00:15:42,433 --> 00:15:45,067
So you can turn away to the patient.

197
00:15:45,567 --> 00:15:49,967
And again, one of the things we were very
concerned about is that doctors didn't

198
00:15:50,033 --> 00:15:52,233
have to pay too much attention.

199
00:15:52,767 --> 00:15:59,367
We tried to make the forms actually as big
as we could given the screens of the day.

200
00:15:59,433 --> 00:16:02,333
There's no question
that we wanted bigger screens.

201
00:16:03,433 --> 00:16:06,567
I suspect everybody always
wants bigger screens,

202
00:16:06,633 --> 00:16:10,333
though there have been exceptions to this.

203
00:16:11,967 --> 00:16:18,367
We had some long discussions with Patrice
Degoulet in France, who had a system

204
00:16:18,433 --> 00:16:24,333
in which they tried to cram as much
information as humanly possible on a form

205
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:27,367
because people didn't wanna
change the forms very often.

206
00:16:27,900 --> 00:16:31,833
And that was in a hospital setting
and was in a quite different setting.

207
00:16:31,900 --> 00:16:33,300
We went the other way.

208
00:16:33,367 --> 00:16:37,667
We were trying to keep each
individual form as simple as possible.

209
00:16:38,200 --> 00:16:42,367
That one doesn't have any further
levels but you, you could get

210
00:16:42,933 --> 00:16:47,033
a third level on some things.
If, if you cancel what you have there,

211
00:16:47,100 --> 00:16:52,033
or accept it, and you go
to more on right thumb.

212
00:16:53,933 --> 00:16:59,000
Yes, you see there, the right thumb
joint, you still get another more,

213
00:17:00,300 --> 00:17:01,933
and you can keep going.

214
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:06,900
Now the other thing about these forms and
the reason you could have so many of them

215
00:17:06,967 --> 00:17:10,367
is they weren't built
individually in advance.

216
00:17:10,933 --> 00:17:12,800
All of the forms were built

217
00:17:12,867 --> 00:17:17,333
out of fragmentary forms
that were assembled on the fly.

218
00:17:18,333 --> 00:17:23,767
And a great deal of the trick
was the techniques that we used

219
00:17:23,833 --> 00:17:28,567
took a long time to build
and the classification process

220
00:17:28,633 --> 00:17:33,067
that was essential to making this work,
was very slow at the time.

221
00:17:34,967 --> 00:17:38,733
But the retrieval technique
