00:19:28,367 --> 00:19:32,067
we had to be efficient for the,
reasonably efficient for the authors.

247
00:19:32,133 --> 00:19:34,233
The authors of the logical tree or?

248
00:19:34,300 --> 00:19:38,867
after you made the change,
you then looked at it.

249
00:19:40,100 --> 00:19:43,533
You probably, you had to go and have
a cup of coffee at those days.

250
00:19:44,233 --> 00:19:49,567
The process was look at what you want,
write down what has to be changed,

251
00:19:49,633 --> 00:19:54,500
or more likely talk to the doctors in a
workshop, find out what they did like,

252
00:19:55,733 --> 00:19:59,633
find out what had to be changed,
make the change, go

253
00:20:00,400 --> 00:20:05,033
set the thing off to compile,
classify and reorganize itself,

254
00:20:05,100 --> 00:20:08,500
have a cup of coffee or whatever
else you were going to do,

255
00:20:08,567 --> 00:20:11,467
and then come back and see
if you got the result you wanted.

256
00:20:11,533 --> 00:20:17,667
And chances were you didn't.
And so you then try it again.

257
00:20:18,433 --> 00:20:22,400
And like any other
software debugging process,

258
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:28,167
you eventually got the knack of getting
better and better at how you hung

259
00:20:28,233 --> 00:20:32,200
these things together so that that they
came out the way you wanted them to.

260
00:20:33,167 --> 00:20:38,867
And the logic of this worked very well.
And if you got something really weird,

261
00:20:39,933 --> 00:20:43,967
and we did get some really
weird things from time to time,

262
00:20:44,033 --> 00:20:47,433
It probably meant that there
was something wrong in the ontology,

263
00:20:48,300 --> 00:20:49,967
something really wrong in the ontology.

264
00:20:50,033 --> 00:20:55,067
And all it takes is getting a little bit
